	In problems 4#, invert the matrix.
	
	#. $$[[4, 1 ; -1, 2]].$$

	**Solution:**

	$$
		\frac{1}{9} [[ 2, -1 ; 1, 4 ]].
	$$

	#. $$[[1, 0, -1 ; 4, 5, 6 ; 0, -1, 2]].$$

	**Solution:**

	$$
		\frac{1}{20} [[ 16, 1, 5 ; -8, 2, -10 ; -4, 1, 5 ]].
	$$

	#. $$[[2, 1, 3 ; 3, -1, 2 ; 1, 0, 1]].$$

	**Solution:** This matrix is not invertible!

	#. $$[[4]].$$

	**Solution:**

	$$
		[[ \frac{1}{4} ]].
	$$


	- - -

	#. Let $$A = [[a, b ; c, d]]$$ be a generic $2 \times 2$ matrix. Use **row reduction** (not a 2x2 inverse formula) to find a formula for $A^{-1}$ in terms of $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$. What condition does this enforce on $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$?

	**Solution:** This solution implicitly assumes that $a$ and $c$ are nonzero, but it is very similar if they are.

	$$
		[[ a, b | 1, 0 ; c, d | 0, 1 ]] &

		[[ a, b | 1, 0 ; ac, ad | 0, a ]] & \qquad \vec{r_2} \to a\vec{r_2}

		[[ a, b | 1, 0 ; 0, ad - bc | -c, a ]] & \qquad \vec{r_2} \to \vec{r_2} - c\vec{r_1}
	$$

	If $ad - bc = 0$, then the bottom row of the left side is entirely zero, so $A$ can't be inverted. Otherwise, we can multiply through by it to clear the last entry of $\vec{r_1}$:

	$$
		[[ a(ad - bc), b(ad - bc) | ad - bc, 0 ; 0, ad - bc | -c, a ]] & \qquad \vec{r_1} \to (ad - bc)\vec{r_1}

		[[ a(ad - bc), 0 | ad, -ab ; 0, ad - bc | -c, a ]] & \qquad \vec{r_1} \to \vec{r_1} - b\vec{r_2}

		[[ ad - bc, 0 | d, -b ; 0, ad - bc | -c, a ]] & \qquad \vec{r_1} \to \frac{1}{a}\vec{r_1}
	$$

	Therefore,

	$$
		A^{-1} = \frac{1}{ad - bc} [[ d, -b ; -c, a ]].
	$$



